# Postern — example environment. Copy to `.env` and edit; every line here is OPTIONAL and the
# commented value is what ships. docker-compose.yml reads this file for ${VAR} interpolation ONLY and
# then delivers the values to the container; the gateway itself has no dotenv dependency and never
# reads a .env file, so a variable that has no line in docker-compose.yml's `environment:` block can
# never reach it no matter what you write here (that was issue #97).
#
# ⚠️ This file is a template. Your real `.env` holds client secrets and the vault master key — it is
# gitignored and must stay that way.

# ── Database ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Postgres (local dev via docker compose). This is the APP/DEV database (pci) — your real data.
# The test suite must NEVER run against it: many DB-gated tests mutate and DELETE rows. Point the
# suite at a SEPARATE, disposable database whose name ends in "_test" (pci_test) — `npm run test:db`
# creates/migrates/runs it for you. A fail-closed guard (vitest.globalSetup.ts, ADR 0015) ABORTS the
# run if DATABASE_URL names a non-"_test" database.
# ⚠️ PCI_TEST_DB=1 disables that guard completely. DO NOT SET IT — nothing needs it. It exists only
# as an emergency override, and running the suite against a real database is how ~700 finance rows
# were deleted on 2026-06-17. `npm run test:db` is the supported path and never requires it.
#
# Running under docker compose? This value is IGNORED there — the app service builds its own
# DATABASE_URL from POSTGRES_USER / POSTGRES_PASSWORD / POSTGRES_DB below (pointing at `db:5432` on
# the compose network). This line is for running the gateway or the tools directly on your machine,
# and if you change the POSTGRES_* knobs you must change it to match.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://pci:pci@localhost:5432/pci

# The compose `db` service's role / password / database name. Defaults: pci / pci / pci. Postgres
# only honours these on FIRST boot — they initialise the data directory — so changing them against an
# existing pgdata volume points the app at a role that does not exist rather than renaming anything.
# Set them before your first `docker compose up`, or leave them alone. The app service's DATABASE_URL
# is built from these same three values, so one edit keeps both services agreeing.
#
# ⚠️ KEEP THE PASSWORD ALPHANUMERIC. These values are substituted into a connection URL as literal
# text — compose cannot percent-encode them — so a password containing / : @ ? # or & produces a URL
# that parses wrong or not at all. The failure is horrible to diagnose: the gateway retries a URL it
# cannot parse for about two and a half minutes and then exits with "Invalid URL", which looks
# nothing like "my password has a slash in it".
# Generate one that is safe by construction — hex has no special characters at all:
#   openssl rand -hex 24
# Do NOT use `openssl rand -base64 32` for THIS value: base64's alphabet includes `/` and `+`, so
# roughly two thirds of generated passwords contain a character that breaks the URL. (base64 is
# still exactly right for PCI_MASTER_KEY further down — that one is read as a value, not a URL.)
# POSTGRES_USER=pci
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD=pci
# POSTGRES_DB=pci

# Where Postgres is published ON YOUR MACHINE (loopback only), for psql / backups / the test DB.
# Purely a host-side knob: the app reaches Postgres over the compose network at db:5432 either way,
# so moving this never affects the gateway. Change it if something else already holds 5432.
# POSTGRES_HOST_PORT=5432

# ── Ports and binding ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The two ports this gateway listens on (OPTIONAL — the defaults below are what ships).
# REST_PORT is the Console + admin/OAuth surface; open http://localhost:<REST_PORT> in a browser.
# MCP_HTTP_PORT is the remote MCP edge an agent harness connects to at /mcp (bearer-gated).
# Both are published on 127.0.0.1 ONLY, 1:1 (the port inside the container is the port on your
# machine), so a value set here is the address you actually type. Change them if something else on
# your machine already holds 8787 / 8788 — that is the whole reason they are knobs and not literals.
# ⚠️ Moving REST_PORT moves the OAuth redirect this gateway sends to Google / Microsoft / WHOOP: it
# becomes http://localhost:<REST_PORT>/api/oauth/<provider>/callback, and providers EXACT-MATCH it.
# Re-register that URI in your provider app, or consent will fail with a redirect_uri mismatch.
# REST_PORT=8787
# MCP_HTTP_PORT=8788

# ── THE ONE SECURITY DECISION IN THIS FILE: WHICH ADDRESS YOU PUBLISH ON ───────────────────────────
#
# Postern's port 8787 serves the Console and the admin surface together: onboarding, credential
# paste, audit read, and the route that MINTS AN AGENT KEY with access to every sector you have
# granted. That group has NO per-request authentication — no bearer, no password, no session. What
# guards it is a Host-header allowlist, and the Host header is a string the client chooses. Anyone
# who can reach the port can send `Host: localhost:8787` and mint a key. One request, no credentials,
# and the key comes back in the response. The allowlist is a DNS-rebinding guard (it stops a web page
# on someone else's site from reaching your gateway through your browser); it is not authentication
# and was never meant to be.
#
# So the whole boundary is the ADDRESS THE PORT IS PUBLISHED ON. Publish it loopback-scoped:
#
#     SAFE      docker run -p 127.0.0.1:8787:8787 …        reachable only from this machine
#     UNSAFE    docker run -p 8787:8787 …                  reachable from your whole network
#
# The bare form is not "the default"; it means 0.0.0.0 — every interface. On Linux, Docker's publish
# rules sit ahead of most host firewalls, so a `ufw deny 8787` does NOT save you. docker-compose.yml
# in this repo already publishes 127.0.0.1-only, and that prefix is the reason it is safe to run.
#
# TO REACH THE CONSOLE FROM ANOTHER MACHINE, do not move the publish address. Put something that
# authenticates in front of it and have that terminate on loopback: `tailscale serve`, an SSH tunnel,
# or a reverse proxy that authenticates before forwarding. The port stays local; the tunnel carries
# the identity. The MCP edge (8788) is different — it is bearer-gated, which IS authentication, and
# it is the surface designed for remote agents; remote exposure for it is turned on from the Console
# (Settings → Remote access), not by editing a publish address here.
#
# The two variables below are a DIFFERENT question: which interface the process binds INSIDE
# whatever it is running in. Two different right answers, and neither is the boundary above:
#   * In Docker the image already sets both to 0.0.0.0, and it must — a container-loopback bind makes
#     the published port reach nothing at all. Safety comes from the publish address, not from this.
#   * Outside Docker (`npm start`) the code's own default is 127.0.0.1, and that is the one to keep,
#     because with no container in the way this bind IS the publish. Setting it to 0.0.0.0 on a bare
#     host is the same exposure as the UNSAFE line above.
# REST_HOST=127.0.0.1
# MCP_HTTP_HOST=127.0.0.1

# Extra values the `Host:` header may carry on the no-bearer admin/OAuth routes — comma-separated.
# localhost / 127.0.0.1 / [::1] are always accepted and need no entry here; add a name only when you
# reach the Console through a hostname the gateway would otherwise reject (e.g. a tunnel's name).
# ⚠️ This is a same-origin / DNS-rebinding guard, NOT authentication. Adding a name widens which
# requests are ACCEPTED; it adds no identity check, so it does not make the admin surface safe to
# publish. Reach the Console from another machine over an authenticated tunnel terminating on
# loopback (e.g. `tailscale serve`) rather than by opening the port.
# REST_ALLOWED_HOSTS=

# Where the built Console lives. Unset = `apps/web/dist` resolved relative to the server's own
# module, which is right in the image and in a git checkout. Set it only if you serve a Console built
# somewhere else; a missing directory is announced with one line on stderr at boot.
# PCI_CONSOLE_DIR=

# NOTE: connector secrets (OAuth tokens, Home Assistant tokens, SimpleFIN access URLs)
# are stored ENCRYPTED in the secrets vault and referenced by secret_ref on the
# connections table. They never live in this file or in any data row.

# Secret vault master key (OPTIONAL). If unset, the gateway autogenerates and persists a key at
# .pci/master.key (mode 0600) on first run — zero-config. Set PCI_MASTER_KEY to manage the key
# yourself (e.g. host-separated from the DB): base64 of EXACTLY 32 bytes (AES-256).
#   openssl rand -base64 32
# The value set here is delivered to the container by docker-compose.yml's app service, and it must
# be set BEFORE first boot: once the vault holds rows sealed under the autogenerated key, pointing a
# different key at it makes the gateway refuse to start (that refusal is the point — it is what stops
# a mismatched key booting green and failing at the first sync).
# PCI_MASTER_KEY=
#
# Optional label for the env-provided key (defaults to 'env'). The comment MUST stay on its own line:
# compose's dotenv parser only strips a trailing comment when a VALUE precedes it, so uncommenting
# `PCI_MASTER_KEY_ID=   # optional label` would deliver the literal comment text AS the key id and
# seal every credential under it. Changing it later then looks like a lost key at boot.
# PCI_MASTER_KEY_ID=
#
# Where the AUTOGENERATED key file is written when PCI_MASTER_KEY is unset. Default `.pci/master.key`
# (i.e. /app/.pci/master.key in the container). ⚠️ The pci_master_key volume is mounted at /app/.pci:
# point this outside that directory and the key stops surviving restarts, which makes every sealed
# credential undecryptable on the next boot. Leave it unset unless you are moving the mount too.
# PCI_MASTER_KEY_PATH=

# Home Assistant instance URL (OPTIONAL) — the push connector's websocket target, interpolated into
# docker-compose's app service. Per-instance: your HA address as a LAN IP (NOT mDNS .local, which
# won't resolve inside a container). Leave unset to skip the HA connector.
#   (Per-connection URL in the Console is the real fix — tracked in issue #3.)
# HA_URL=http://192.168.1.50:8123

# OAuth bridge for strict hosted MCP clients (claude.ai/ChatGPT) — ADR 0029.
# SETTING THIS IS THE SWITCH: the MCP edge serves OAuth discovery + a paste-your-agent-key
# authorize page at this exact public origin (must be https; hosted clients also need the
# gateway publicly reachable, e.g. tailscale funnel — see docs/runbooks/remote-access.md).
# Unset = bridge off; the edge is static-bearer-only, byte-identical to pre-bridge behavior.
# MCP_PUBLIC_URL=
#
# Trusted reverse proxies in front of the MCP edge (ADR 0035 §D3 item 1). Delivered to the container
# by docker-compose's app service; the edge decides how to read it. Unset = no proxy is trusted.
# MCP_TRUSTED_PROXIES=

# Where YOUR OWN browser lands coming back from a provider's consent screen — the exact
# `redirect_uri` this gateway sends to Google / Microsoft / WHOOP (ADR 0035 §D4).
# LEAVE THIS UNSET unless you know you need it: unset (or empty) means http://localhost:8787, which
# is the literal every user registers with their own provider app, and it works on every machine.
# This is NOT MCP_PUBLIC_URL — that one is where a vendor's cloud fleet reaches this box, and the two
# are deliberately different settings. It must NEVER be a *.ts.net tailnet name: Google requires
# DNS-level ownership of the redirect's domain and nobody owns ts.net but Tailscale (§D5).
# Set it only if you serve this gateway on a domain YOU own and registered that redirect there.
# PCI_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=
#
# Where the browser lands INSIDE the Console after a SUCCESSFUL consent. Unset = the Console's own
# sources page, which is right whenever you are serving the bundled Console. Set it only if you front
# this gateway with your own UI. There is deliberately no failure twin: a failed consent always lands
# on the provider's connect screen, so a wrong value here can never swallow an error.
# ⚠️ SET A PATH OR LEAVE IT COMMENTED — never uncomment it with nothing after the `=`. An empty value
# is not read as "unset" here: it is used as the redirect base, so every completed consent lands on
# `/` instead of the page that shows the credential it just created, and the consent looks like it
# did nothing. That exact dead end was issue #96. (Tracked for a code-side fix as #130.)
# PCI_CONSOLE_SUCCESS_URL=/sources

# ── Provider apps (OAuth client credentials) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# You register your OWN app with each provider and paste its credentials — this gateway ships no
# shared client. The FIRST-CLASS way to supply them is the Console (Settings / the provider's connect
# screen): those are stored in the database with the secret sealed in the vault, and they WIN over
# anything set here. These variables are the headless fallback for a deploy that never visits a UI.
#
# Redirect URI to register with every provider: http://localhost:<REST_PORT>/api/oauth/<provider>/callback
# (exact match — see the REST_PORT warning above).
#
# Google is a CONFIDENTIAL client: the token exchange fails without the secret.
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
#
# WHOOP is a CONFIDENTIAL client: same — the secret is required.
# WHOOP_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
# WHOOP_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
#
# Microsoft is a PUBLIC (PKCE) client — register it as such and leave the secret unset. The variable
# exists only because the same resolution path reads it; supplying one is not required and not needed.
# MICROSOFT_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
# MICROSOFT_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
#
# Plaid (bank connections) — your own Plaid app. Same rule: the Console's Plaid app wins over these.
# PLAID_ENV picks the API host and ONLY the exact string "production" leaves the sandbox, so unset is
# the safe default. Missing id/secret is a clear error at connect time, never a silent misconfigure.
# PLAID_CLIENT_ID=
# PLAID_SECRET=
# PLAID_ENV=sandbox

# ── Diagnostics and tuning (rarely needed) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Dump each authenticated push-ingest body to a directory in the container for offline shape-diffing
# (issue #29). Temporary diagnostics — the bodies are real data, so unset it again when you are done.
# PCI_INGEST_DEBUG_DIR=/tmp/pci-ingest-debug
#
# Base backoff in milliseconds when a provider answers a sync with a 5xx. Unset = the built-in value.
# PCI_RETRY_5XX_BASE_MS=
#
# Host port for the opt-in log viewer (`docker compose --profile debug up -d logs`), loopback only.
# POSTGRES_HOST_PORT's twin: it moves where YOU reach it and nothing else.
# DOZZLE_HOST_PORT=9999
